-- Enrolling a participant into a clinical trial - the moment a person
-- is "randomised" into a drug study - with the paperwork checked the way
-- a regulator will check it years later.
--
-- A quick tour of the domain, in case clinical trials are not your
-- world. Before a participant can be randomised, several documents and
-- approvals all have to be in force on that exact day: the protocol (the
-- master plan for the study, approved by an ethics committee), the
-- consent form the person signed, the doctor's delegated authority to
-- enrol people, and a recent lab result showing the person is eligible.
-- Each of these is valid only between two dates - a "from" date and a
-- "to" date - and outside that window it simply does not count. A
-- consent form approved through 31 March is no good on 1 April; a lab
-- result expires once its stability window passes. The question an
-- inspector asks, sometimes years afterwards, is always the same: *was
-- this person legitimately randomised on this date, under everything
-- that was valid that day?* That should be one lookup, not a forensic
-- dig across four separate archives.
--
-- Most of the rules below are validity-window checks. We write each one
-- as two halves joined with `and`: the window's start is on-or-before
-- the action date, and the action date is on-or-before the window's end.
-- Read `x on_or_before y` simply as "the date x falls on or before the
-- date y". Both ends are inclusive, so a document valid "through 31
-- March" is still valid on 31 March itself.
--
-- Here is how Morpholog thinks about all this. It does not store
-- "facts" - it stores *claims* it has agreed to admit because they obey
-- the rules. Two kinds of rule do the work:
--
--   * an `invariant` is a rule that must hold for all admitted state,
--     forever. The runtime refuses to commit any change that would
--     break one.
--   * a `require` is a gate on a single action - a condition checked at
--     the moment you act, and never again.
--
-- The distinction is load-bearing here. The validity windows are gates,
-- checked the day a participant is randomised. They are deliberately
-- NOT invariants. A week after an enrolment, the sponsor may close the
-- old protocol and approve a new one - and that must not reach back and
-- invalidate an enrolment that was perfectly valid when it happened.
-- "What did we admit, under the rules in force then?" must always have
-- an honest answer. Closing a window governs the future, never the past.

program clinical_trial_enrolment

-- The vocabulary. Each `predicate` names a kind of claim this world can
-- hold and the shape of its arguments. These are the only nouns the
-- system knows: the trial itself, the dated documents and approvals
-- (protocol versions, consent forms, ethics sign-offs), the people
-- involved (screened participants, delegated investigators), the
-- eligibility criteria and the lab assessments against them, any open
-- protocol deviation that blocks a participant, and finally the
-- randomisation record itself. The dates on a version or assessment are
-- its validity window: the period over which that thing is in force.
predicate Trial(trial_id: Subject)
predicate ProtocolVersion(trial_id: Subject, protocol_version: Subject, effective_from: Date, effective_to: Date)
predicate ProtocolApprovedBy(protocol_version: Subject, ethics_committee: Subject, approval_id: Subject)
predicate ConsentFormVersion(trial_id: Subject, consent_form_version: Subject, effective_from: Date, effective_to: Date)
predicate ConsentFormApprovedBy(consent_form_version: Subject, ethics_committee: Subject, approval_id: Subject)
predicate ParticipantScreened(participant_id: Subject, trial_id: Subject, screened_on: Date)
predicate InformedConsentObtained(participant_id: Subject, trial_id: Subject, consent_form_version: Subject, consented_on: Date, obtained_by: Subject)
predicate EligibilityCriterion(protocol_version: Subject, criterion_id: Subject, required_result: Subject)
predicate EligibilityAssessment(participant_id: Subject, criterion_id: Subject, result: Subject, assessed_on: Date, expires_on: Date)
predicate DelegatedInvestigator(investigator: Subject, trial_id: Subject, role: Subject, effective_from: Date, effective_to: Date)
predicate ImportantProtocolDeviationOpen(participant_id: Subject, trial_id: Subject, deviation_id: Subject)
predicate ParticipantRandomised(participant_id: Subject, trial_id: Subject, protocol_version: Subject, randomised_on: Date, randomised_by: Subject)

-- Intents are messages the system sends to the outside world once a
-- change commits - "a trial opened", "a participant was randomised".
-- They are how the trial tells the rest of the machinery (the
-- randomisation system, the case-report-form database) what just
-- happened.
intent TrialOpened(trial_id: Subject)
intent ProtocolVersionApproved(trial_id: Subject, protocol_version: Subject)
intent ConsentFormVersionApproved(trial_id: Subject, consent_form_version: Subject)
intent InvestigatorDelegated(investigator: Subject, trial_id: Subject, role: Subject)
intent ParticipantScreened(participant_id: Subject, trial_id: Subject)
intent InformedConsentObtained(participant_id: Subject, consent_form_version: Subject)
intent EligibilityCriterionRecorded(protocol_version: Subject, criterion_id: Subject)
intent EligibilityAssessmentRecorded(participant_id: Subject, criterion_id: Subject)
intent ImportantProtocolDeviationOpened(participant_id: Subject, trial_id: Subject, deviation_id: Subject)
intent ParticipantRandomised(participant_id: Subject)


-- The rules that must always hold. If any change would leave the world
-- violating one of these, the whole change is rejected at commit.

-- A protocol version can have only one validity window. If the same
-- protocol version were allowed two different "from/to" windows, you
-- could quietly change after the fact whether an earlier enrolment fell
-- inside it. Pinning one window per version keeps the past honest.
invariant at_most_one_protocol_window_per_version:
    ProtocolVersion(trial, version, from_a, to_a) and ProtocolVersion(trial, version, from_b, to_b) implies (from_a = from_b and to_a = to_b)

-- Same rule for consent form versions: one validity window per version.
invariant at_most_one_consent_window_per_version:
    ConsentFormVersion(trial, version, from_a, to_a) and ConsentFormVersion(trial, version, from_b, to_b) implies (from_a = from_b and to_a = to_b)

-- A participant is randomised at most once in a given trial. Two
-- randomisation records for the same person in the same trial must
-- agree on every detail - same protocol version, same date, same
-- enrolling doctor. This catches the "accidentally enrolled twice"
-- error without making the validity windows themselves eternal.
invariant participant_randomised_once_per_trial:
    ParticipantRandomised(p, trial, proto_a, date_a, actor_a) and ParticipantRandomised(p, trial, proto_b, date_b, actor_b) implies (proto_a = proto_b and date_a = date_b and actor_a = actor_b)

-- Consent must come before randomisation. For every randomised
-- participant there must be a recorded consent whose signing date is
-- on or before the randomisation date - you cannot enrol someone who
-- had not yet agreed. This one IS an invariant rather than a one-time
-- gate: even if a later correction moves a randomisation to a new date,
-- the consent must still cover that new date. The order "consented,
-- then randomised" must hold over all admitted state, forever.
invariant consent_obtained_before_randomisation:
    ParticipantRandomised(p, t, _, randomised_on, _) implies (exists consented_on: InformedConsentObtained(p, t, _, consented_on, _) and consented_on on_or_before randomised_on)



-- The ways state may change. A transformation proposes a set of
-- changes; the runtime applies them only if every invariant still holds
-- once they are in place. `admit` adds a claim; `require` is the gate
-- that must pass before the change goes through.

-- Setup steps - just enough to make a real randomisation reachable.
-- Each piece of the evidence (the ethics-approved protocol, the consent
-- form, the doctor's delegated authority, the eligibility lab result)
-- arrives as its own recorded step rather than being conjured as a side
-- effect of enrolment, exactly as it happens in a real trial.
transformation open_trial(trial_id):
    admit Trial(trial_id)
    emit TrialOpened(trial_id)

transformation approve_protocol_version(
    trial_id,
    protocol_version,
    effective_from,
    effective_to,
    ethics_committee,
    approval_id
):
    require Trial(trial_id)
    admit ProtocolVersion(trial_id, protocol_version, effective_from, effective_to)
    admit ProtocolApprovedBy(protocol_version, ethics_committee, approval_id)
    emit ProtocolVersionApproved(trial_id, protocol_version)

transformation approve_consent_form_version(
    trial_id,
    consent_form_version,
    effective_from,
    effective_to,
    ethics_committee,
    approval_id
):
    require Trial(trial_id)
    admit ConsentFormVersion(trial_id, consent_form_version, effective_from, effective_to)
    admit ConsentFormApprovedBy(consent_form_version, ethics_committee, approval_id)
    emit ConsentFormVersionApproved(trial_id, consent_form_version)

transformation delegate_investigator(
    investigator,
    trial_id,
    role,
    effective_from,
    effective_to
):
    require Trial(trial_id)
    admit DelegatedInvestigator(investigator, trial_id, role, effective_from, effective_to)
    emit InvestigatorDelegated(investigator, trial_id, role)

transformation screen_participant(participant_id, trial_id, screened_on):
    require Trial(trial_id)
    admit ParticipantScreened(participant_id, trial_id, screened_on)
    emit ParticipantScreened(participant_id, trial_id)

transformation record_consent(
    participant_id,
    trial_id,
    consent_form_version,
    consented_on,
    obtained_by
):
    require Trial(trial_id)
    admit InformedConsentObtained(
        participant_id,
        trial_id,
        consent_form_version,
        consented_on,
        obtained_by
    )
    emit InformedConsentObtained(participant_id, consent_form_version)

transformation record_eligibility_criterion(protocol_version, criterion_id, required_result):
    admit EligibilityCriterion(protocol_version, criterion_id, required_result)
    emit EligibilityCriterionRecorded(protocol_version, criterion_id)

transformation record_eligibility_assessment(
    participant_id,
    criterion_id,
    result,
    assessed_on,
    expires_on
):
    admit EligibilityAssessment(participant_id, criterion_id, result, assessed_on, expires_on)
    emit EligibilityAssessmentRecorded(participant_id, criterion_id)

transformation open_important_protocol_deviation(participant_id, trial_id, deviation_id):
    require Trial(trial_id)
    admit ImportantProtocolDeviationOpen(participant_id, trial_id, deviation_id)
    emit ImportantProtocolDeviationOpened(participant_id, trial_id, deviation_id)

-- The heart of the example: actually randomising a participant. This
-- goes through only if EVERY piece of evidence is in force on the
-- randomisation date - the protocol window covers it, the consent form
-- window covers it, the signed consent precedes it, the doctor's
-- delegation covers it, the eligibility lab result is recorded, matches
-- the required result, and has not expired - and there is no open
-- protocol deviation blocking the participant. If any single check
-- fails, the whole enrolment is refused. The doctor passes in which
-- protocol version they are enrolling under, since they know it.
transformation randomise_participant(
    participant_id,
    trial_id,
    protocol_version,
    randomised_on
):
    -- Every condition below is part of one big gate, joined with `and`.
    -- Each validity window appears as two lines: the window opens on or
    -- before the randomisation date, and the randomisation date is on or
    -- before the window closes. All of it must hold, or the enrolment is
    -- turned away.
    require (
        Trial(trial_id)
        and ProtocolVersion(trial_id, protocol_version, protocol_from, protocol_to)
        and protocol_from on_or_before randomised_on
        and randomised_on on_or_before protocol_to
        and ProtocolApprovedBy(protocol_version, _, _)
        and ConsentFormVersion(trial_id, consent_form_version, consent_from, consent_to)
        and consent_from on_or_before randomised_on
        and randomised_on on_or_before consent_to
        and ConsentFormApprovedBy(consent_form_version, _, _)
        and InformedConsentObtained(participant_id, trial_id, consent_form_version, consented_on, _)
        and consented_on on_or_before randomised_on
        and DelegatedInvestigator(actor, trial_id, #randomise_participant, del_from, del_to)
        and del_from on_or_before randomised_on
        and randomised_on on_or_before del_to
        and EligibilityCriterion(protocol_version, criterion_id, required_result)
        and EligibilityAssessment(participant_id, criterion_id, required_result, assessed_on, expires_on)
        and assessed_on on_or_before randomised_on
        and randomised_on on_or_before expires_on
        and not ImportantProtocolDeviationOpen(participant_id, trial_id, _)
    )

    admit ParticipantRandomised(participant_id, trial_id, protocol_version, randomised_on, actor)
    emit ParticipantRandomised(participant_id)
